{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Hom.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":18,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.crossFactor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.crossFactor","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L170-L181","doc":"**Cross-anchor connecting factor is a pure phase.** For two σ-unitaries\n`u₁, u₂` (the fibre-saddle `Φ_b` at the proton and muon anchors), the\nconnecting factor `w = σ(u₁)·u₂` is itself σ-unitary and satisfies\n`u₁·w = u₂`. Hence *all* cross-anchor variation between σ-unitaries is\ncarried by a σ-unitary (pure-phase) factor — the magnitude channel is\nflat, and the `Δc_3` swing is a phase-channel (bar-odd) quantity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.inv","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L163-L168","doc":"The σ-inverse of a σ-unitary is σ-unitary. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.mul_bar_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.mul_bar_self","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L157-L161","doc":"The σ-conjugate `σ u` is the two-sided inverse of a σ-unitary `u`\n(commutative form of `u·ū = 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.not_bar_even_of_sq_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.not_bar_even_of_sq_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L110-L117","doc":"**Phases are bar-odd (mod involutions).** A σ-unitary with `u² ≠ 1`\ncannot be bar-even: it carries a nontrivial bar-odd part. The algebraic\nreason the fibre-saddle pure-phase `Φ_b` lives on the bar-odd (decay)\nface. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.sq_eq_one_of_bar_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.sq_eq_one_of_bar_even","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L102-L108","doc":"**Bar-even unitaries are involutions.** If `u` is σ-unitary and σ-fixed\n(bar-even), then `u² = 1`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:phi-b-unitary-bar-odd","label":"prop:phi-b-unitary-bar-odd","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/phi-b-unitary-bar-odd.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary.mul","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L93-L100","doc":"σ-unitaries are closed under multiplication. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.isSigmaUnitary_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.isSigmaUnitary_one","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L89-L91","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsSigmaUnitary","kind":"def","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L85-L87","doc":"σ-unitary: `σ(u)·u = 1` — the ring-involution form of `|u| = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.c_eq_phi_zero_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.c_eq_phi_zero_sq","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L62-L66","doc":"Derived: the Faddeev inversion constant is `Φ_b(0)^2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.inversion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.inversion","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L60-L60","doc":"Faddeev inversion relation `Φ(x)·Φ(-x) = c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.c","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.c","kind":"def","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L58-L58","doc":"The Faddeev inversion constant (the archimedean instance is\n`Φ_b(0)^2 · e^{πix²}`; here the constant `c`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.modular_invariance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.modular_invariance","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L55-L55","doc":"Modular invariance `Φ_b = Φ_{b^{-1}}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.bar_invol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.bar_invol","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L53-L53","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.bar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.bar","kind":"def","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L52-L52","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.Phi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.Phi","kind":"def","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L51-L51","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble.mk","kind":"ctor","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L50-L50","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FaddeevModularDouble","kind":"structure","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L45-L60","doc":"Structural axiomatisation of the Faddeev modular-double quantum\ndilogarithm `Φ_b`. The transcendental function is not constructed\n(open); its defining identities are recorded so downstream results\ndepend on them explicitly. `bar` is the modular involution `b ↦ b^{-1}`\n(`= q ↦ q^{-1}`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bar_parity_decomposition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bar_parity_decomposition","kind":"theorem","line":30,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FaddeevModularDouble.lean#L30-L43","doc":"**Bar-parity decomposition** (the sinh/cosh split under the modular\ninvolution). Over any commutative ring, for a ring involution `σ` (the\nbar `q ↦ q^{-1}` / modular double `b ↦ b^{-1}`), the doubled element\n`2·f` splits as a **bar-even** (cosh; mass/Rogers face) part `f + σ f`,\nfixed by `σ`, plus a **bar-odd** (sinh; decay/CS face) part `f − σ f`,\nnegated by `σ`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:phi-b-bar-parity-decomposition","label":"prop:phi-b-bar-parity-decomposition","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/phi-b-bar-parity-decomposition.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}